# Force LF line endings for the vendored blamejs tree.
#
# Without this, git's autocrlf=true on Windows checks out vendor/blamejs/
# files with CRLF line endings, while Linux/macOS checkouts (and CI runners)
# get LF. Our boot-time integrity gate (`b.configDrift.verifyVendorIntegrity`
# in bin/hermitstash-sync.js) hashes filesystem bytes against the recorded
# SHA256 in vendor/MANIFEST.json — line-ending drift means the hashes
# computed on a Windows dev machine don't match the bytes a Linux CI
# runner sees, and the integrity check fails on the workflow's first
# `hermitstash-sync version` invocation.
#
# Pinning the vendor tree to LF makes Windows + Linux + macOS see
# identical bytes regardless of autocrlf, which is the only way the
# hash-recorded-against-disk-bytes contract holds cross-platform.
vendor/blamejs/** text eol=lf
vendor/blamejs/lib/vendor/*.cjs text eol=lf

# All JavaScript is LF on every platform. scripts/ + bin/ are size-sensitive
# (vendor-hash.js and anything that hashes them must not drift), and a
# Windows-authored lib/ or tests/ file picked up with CRLF otherwise lands a
# whole-file EOL flip in the committed blob (a 2000-line spurious diff for a
# one-line change). Pinning *.js to LF keeps every JS blob byte-identical
# regardless of core.autocrlf.
*.js text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF on every platform. ShellCheck (SC1017) rejects a
# literal carriage return, so a CRLF-committed script breaks the lint gate in
# both ci.yml and docker-publish.yml — and a failed docker-publish lint skips
# the image build entirely. Pin every *.sh to LF regardless of autocrlf so a
# Windows-authored script can never land CRLF bytes in the committed blob.
*.sh text eol=lf
